WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.960
In late 2022, ChatGPT arrived.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.130
You typed a message, and it wrote back.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.910
It could talk, and that was all.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.380
Now there are AI agents that do things.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:17.900
Give one a bug in some code, and it reads the files, runs the tests that check the code, and fixes the bug.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.500
Underneath, it's the same kind of model as the one in the chat.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.040
And a model like that only writes text.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.440
So what sits in between?

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.620
How do you get from a model that writes text, to an agent that fixes a bug?

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.190
Start with the model.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:42.190
It's called an LLM, a large language model, and it does one thing: it predicts the next word, or sometimes a piece of a word.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.630
Give a small, freely available model the words "I made a cup of".

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.110
It gives every word it knows a probability.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.220
"Tea" gets thirty-one percent.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.070
"Coffee", nineteen.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.060
Everything else is far behind.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.010
Pick one of the likely words, and add it to the text.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.080
Then predict again: "tea", then "and", then "I".

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.460
Over and over, one word at a time.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.930
That's all writing is, for an LLM.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.940
It works only from the text in front of it.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.330
That text is called its context.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.440
The model keeps no memory from one use to the next.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.070
So keep two facts in mind.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.750
What comes out of an LLM is text.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.570
It learned a lot in training.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.200
But about your task, it knows only what's in its context.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.420
Here's a task.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.570
A small shop's billing code stops with an error: no price for "gadget".

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.220
You might picture the model reaching into the files itself.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:45.350
So ask a model on its own to fix it: not the small one, but a large model, like the ones behind chat assistants.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.300
In one try, it wrote that it would explore the project, and even wrote out a request to run a command.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.300
But nothing was there to carry it out, and the tests still failed.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.000
Text on its own doesn't do anything.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:02.550
So put a program next to the model, to watch what it writes.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.960
Then give the model a format for requests, described at the top of its context.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.850
A line that starts "search:", then some words.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.530
Or "open file:", then a file name.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.520
Real agents use a stricter format, but the idea is the same.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.610
Now the model writes a line: search, "no price for".

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.180
The program spots the request, and searches the project's files for those words.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:34.340
Each thing the program can do for the model is called a tool: search, open a file, edit a file, run the tests.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.050
Notice who did the searching.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.360
The model only wrote a line of text.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.800
The program did the work.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.190
The search found something.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.770
But the model can't see it.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.820
Remember: it knows only what's in its context.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:52.000
So the program pastes the result into the context.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.320
One match: line eight of a file called prices.py.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.920
Then it runs the model again, on the whole context, because the model remembers nothing.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.820
Now the model continues with the result in view.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.530
It knows something it couldn't have known before: where the error comes from.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.900
So it writes its next request: open that file.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.800
You've probably seen this already.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.160
In 2023, the popular chat assistants started searching the web.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.680
It's the same trick.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:27.170
The model writes a search, a program runs it, and the results are pasted into the context.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.410
Then the model answers, with those pages in view.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.190
One step won't fix a bug.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.180
So repeat.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.390
The model writes a request, the program carries it out, and the result goes into the context.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:41.420
Then again.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:46.020
This goes on until the model writes a reply with no request in it.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.600
That's its answer, and the loop stops.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:53.600
A model working in a loop like this, using tools, is called an agent.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.580
Here's how the whole run went.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.830
After the search and the file you've seen, it searches for where that code is used, and opens that file too.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.700
The price list spells it "Gadget", with a capital G.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.710
So it edits the lookup: now "gadget" finds "Gadget".

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:11.990
Then it runs the tests.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.080
They fail.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.140
Ben's bill should come to thirty-seven.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.090
It comes to thirty-nine fifty.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.820
That failure goes into the context, like any other result, and it changes the next step.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:28.680
The model looks at the test and at Ben's orders, and finds a second mistake.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.570
The bulk discount only kicked in at eleven items.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.110
It should start at ten.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.340
It edits that, and runs the tests again.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.780
This time they pass.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.610
It writes its answer, and the loop stops.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.370
That was twelve requests.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.430
Nobody wrote them down in advance.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.010
Each one followed from the results before it.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.450
If the loop is that simple, why did agents only start working recently?

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.790
People tried, in 2023.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.620
They ran models in loops like this one, and the loops often got stuck, going round in circles.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.440
Part of the problem is that small mistakes add up.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:14.600
Say each step goes right ninety-five times in a hundred, whatever happened before, and nothing catches a mistake.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.730
Start a hundred runs.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.940
About ninety-five get the first step right.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:23.520
Of those, ninety-five percent get the second step right too: about ninety.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.250
Every step keeps ninety-five percent of the runs still on track.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.881
After twenty steps, that's about thirty-six of the hundred.

00:05:32.621 --> 00:05:33.581
About a third.

00:05:34.831 --> 00:05:36.281
Three things changed.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:41.380
First, models were trained to work in this loop, so each step became more reliable.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.940
Suppose that lifts each step to ninety-nine in a hundred.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:50.620
Then about eighty-two of the hundred runs get all twenty steps right: about four in five.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:55.130
Second, tasks like coding come with feedback.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:00.470
A failing test catches a mistake, as it caught the discount, and the next step can fix it.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.390
Third, agents find what they need by searching, step by step, as that run did.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.550
Claude Code, a coding agent, works that way.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.670
Its developers first tried building an index of the code in advance.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.180
But code keeps changing, and an index goes out of date.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.190
They've said searching worked better.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.230
Now put names on the whole picture.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.331
The LLM is the model.

00:06:25.531 --> 00:06:27.711
Everything around it is called the harness.

00:06:28.961 --> 00:06:32.611
The harness writes the starting instructions, with the request format.

00:06:33.001 --> 00:06:37.931
It holds the tools, and the program that carries out each request, and it runs the loop.

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:41.131
A real harness does two more things.

00:06:41.291 --> 00:06:46.881
Before an edit, like the one to the price lookup, it can check permission, sometimes by asking you.

00:06:47.741 --> 00:06:52.471
And every result makes the context longer, but a context can only hold so much.

00:06:52.811 --> 00:06:58.371
When it fills up, the harness trims it, by dropping old results or summarizing older steps.

00:06:59.621 --> 00:07:02.381
So an agent is an LLM plus a harness.

00:07:03.831 --> 00:07:08.511
That's five words to keep: LLM, context, tool, harness, and agent.

00:07:13.461 --> 00:07:18.221
So, how do you get from a model that only writes text to an agent that fixes a bug?

00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:20.181
The model still does one thing.

00:07:20.301 --> 00:07:22.611
It predicts the next word, one at a time.

00:07:22.861 --> 00:07:28.371
The harness turns some of that text into actions, and pastes the results back into the context.

00:07:28.621 --> 00:07:31.551
And the loop turns single steps into a whole job.

00:07:32.601 --> 00:07:35.771
ChatGPT in 2022 could only talk.

00:07:35.941 --> 00:07:37.720
An agent is still talking.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.310
The difference is the harness: a program that listens to what the model writes, and acts on it.
